<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Nysa</h1><p class="st-meta">1290 CE – 1810 CE (approx.) · capital Nysa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Silesian duchy ruled by the bishops of Wrocław from Nysa, the Silesian Rome, a Catholic stronghold for over five centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a> held the castellany of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Nysa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otmuchow">Otmuchów</a> from the 12th century. In 1290 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-probus">Henry IV Probus</a> granted Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-ii-zaremba">Thomas II</a> full princely rights there, making the territory an episcopal duchy. Its bishops took the title Prince of Nysa and in 1342 accepted the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>-dukes ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Nysa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otmuchow">Otmuchów</a> through officials and the estates. Nysa became a wealthy town of churches and convents, called the Silesian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> spread in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Silesia</a>, the bishops led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-of-austria-bishop-of-wroclaw">Archduke Charles of Austria</a>, bishop from 1608, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> college Carolinum. The population was largely German-speaking.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1742 most of the duchy passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, which secularised it in 1810, while the southern part around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesenik">Jeseník</a> remained Austrian under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> until 1850. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Nysa</a> has been Polish since 1945.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-nysa.jpg" alt="Duchy of Nysa" width="320" height="269"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blaeu_1645_-_Silesia_Ducatus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1290 CE – 1810 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nysa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thomas II of Wrocław, Charles of Austria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>, <a href="/countries/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Neisse, Principality of Neisse, Bishopric Duchy of Neisse, Nysa-Otmuchow</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1550&amp;focus=duchy-of-nysa" aria-label="Duchy of Nysa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5076 424 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Nysa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5396 680L5330 692M5396 680L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5330 692h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5396 680h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5396 680h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nysa. <a href="/atlas?year=1550&amp;focus=duchy-of-nysa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

